MV                                                                    MV

NAME
    mv - move or rename files and directories, Unix-style

FORMAT
    mv [-fb] FROM ... TO

TEMPLATE
    none - flags are hand-parsed Unix bundles; `mv ?` prints usage

PATH
    C:mv

FUNCTION
    Moves files, pattern matches, and directories. AmigaDOS
    Rename() is secretly most of mv already - a rename is a
    directory-entry relink, so anything moves anywhere on the SAME
    volume in one cheap call, and mv tries that first, per file.

    When the target is on a DIFFERENT volume, Rename() fails with
    ERROR_RENAME_ACROSS_DEVICES and mv falls back to copy+delete,
    carrying protection bits, datestamp and filenote.

    That fallback covers whole DIRECTORY trees too (since 0.5),
    all-or-nothing per tree: the source is deleted only after the
    ENTIRE tree has copied clean, deepest directory first; any copy
    failure abandons the move, wipes the partial destination, and
    leaves the source untouched. The destination must not already
    exist (merging is cp's business), volume roots are refused as
    sources, and a tree containing a soft link or a hard directory
    link is refused whole - a link cannot be recreated faithfully
    on another volume.

    mv is non-destructive by default: an existing target is
    skipped and listed under "not moved:" (return code 5).

OPTIONS
    -f  force: delete and replace an existing target - SameLock()
        checked first, so `mv -f file file` cannot delete the only
        copy
    -b  back up an existing target as <name>.mvbak first; if that
        backup name is taken the file is refused untouched. -bf
        means "replace the stale .mvbak" - -f always sanctions
        destroying exactly one thing

RESULTS
    Return code 0 when clean, 5 when something was skipped (and
    listed) or a moved source could not be fully deleted, 10 when
    something failed, 20 on Ctrl-C. A break or failure mid-copy
    removes the partial target. More than 32 arguments is an
    error, never a silent truncation.

EXAMPLES
    mv oldname newname
    mv work:file work:archive
    mv work:somedir ram:            (whole tree, cross-volume)
    mv #?.mod mods:
    mv -b config work:app

SEE ALSO
    ls.doc, cp.doc, mkdir.doc, rm.doc, tools-audit.md
